syntax: a parameter expansion token read after an error ends the input

The lexer turns every token into _EOF once p.err is set, so the parser
loops stop at the first error. paramExp and paramExpParameter read the
token after a subscript, an operand or a nested expansion with
paramToken directly and stored it in p.tok without that check. When
paramToken's own p.rune() failed, such as on invalid UTF-8 right after a
`]`, the error was recorded but p.tok kept the token, the error helpers
did nothing more because p.err was already set, and callExpr spun
forever on the same token. These reads now go through nextParamToken,
which applies the same rule as the lexer.

Reproducer, before this change: parse "${0#$[a a]\xf1" with
RecoverErrors(3); Parse never returns.

diff --git a/syntax/lexer.go b/syntax/lexer.go
index eeeb814..bdfc6ce 100644
--- a/syntax/lexer.go
+++ b/syntax/lexer.go
@@ -781,6 +781,13 @@ func (p *Parser) dqToken(r rune) token {
 	panic("unreachable")
 }
 
+func (p *Parser) nextParamToken() {
+	p.tok = p.paramToken(p.r)
+	if p.err != nil {
+		p.tok = _EOF
+	}
+}
+
 func (p *Parser) paramToken(r rune) token {
 	switch r {
 	case '}':
